There is no native `nl2br()` function in JavaScript because the language treats text as a linear stream of characters without inherent awareness of line breaks. In PHP, `nl2br` is a utility that scans a string for newline characters and inserts `<br>` tags at those specific points. While jQuery provides helper functions like `.text()`, `.html()`, or `.replaceWith()`, these operate on DOM nodes rather than raw strings in memory. To replicate the behavior of injecting HTML line breaks into a textarea, you must manually manipulate the string content before rendering it back to the user.

The most straightforward approach involves using standard JavaScript string methods like `split()` and `join()`. This technique assumes the input is a plain string and that you are safe to modify its structure directly. If your goal is to convert newline characters (like `\n` or `\r\n`) into `<br>` tags within a specific context, you can achieve this without relying on event listeners that detect key presses.

Here is a concise pattern using the `split` method:

```javascript
const text = "Line one\nLine two\nLine three";
const htmlWithBreaks = text.split('\n').join('<br>');

console.log(htmlWithBreaks);
// Output: Line one<br>Line two<br>Line three
```

This approach is efficient and readable. However, it requires you to handle the character encoding carefully. The source material mentions detecting `evt.which == 13`, which corresponds to the Enter key press in older browsers. Modern best practices avoid relying on `evt.which` due to inconsistencies across environments; instead, developers should use `evt.key === 'Enter'`.

If you are handling a textarea specifically and want to insert a `<br>` only when the user presses Enter, you must be cautious about how you update the DOM. Directly replacing the selection with an HTML string in a contenteditable element or textarea can lead to cursor jumping issues. The code snippet from the source material attempts to get the selection, replace it with `<BR>`, and then sync the value.

```javascript
$('#TextArea').on('keypress', function (evt) {
    if (evt.key === 'Enter') {
        // This logic is complex because textareas generally store plain text.
        // Injecting HTML tags directly into a textarea often breaks input behavior 
        // unless you are using a contenteditable div instead of a standard <textarea>.

        var range = $('#TextArea').get(0).selectionStart; 
        // Note: getSelection() API is deprecated in many modern contexts.
    }
});
```

A more robust solution for converting newlines to HTML breaks usually happens during the rendering phase, not the input phase. If you are building a rich text editor or a preview pane, it is better to let the user type plain text and then apply `split('\n').join('<br>')` just before displaying the content to the view. This keeps the data model clean (plain text) while allowing the presentation layer to handle formatting.

If you are using Laravel on the backend, you might consider passing a helper function down to your frontend or utilizing Blade directives to sanitize and format output strings server-side if security allows. However, client-side conversion remains the standard for dynamic interactions in the browser. Always remember that inserting `<br>` tags directly into a `<textarea>` element is generally an anti-pattern because textareas are designed to accept raw text, not HTML markup. This distinction is critical when deciding whether to implement `nl2br` logic at all or simply switch to a `contenteditable` div if you need to render formatted text.
